# install targets rebuilt from the paths above on every install or upgrade:
#   ~/.local/neva/{bin,lib,services,plugins,.claude-plugin}, the resources the installed neva reads
#   ~/.claude/rules/neva/common/ and ~/.claude/rules/neva/<lang>/ (enabled plugins only)
#   everything bin/neva install writes into a coding harness: the rules above, the NEVA_*
#   keys inside the env object of ~/.claude/settings.json, the marketplace and plugin
#   registrations it added through the claude CLI, and whatever each
#   adapters/<harness>/install.json names. Every one of those is listed with its checksum and
#   the backup of what it replaced in ~/.local/share/neva/install-state.json, so
#   `neva uninstall` removes exactly them and nothing a buyer wrote. That manifest and
#   ~/.local/share/neva/backups/ are tool-owned, not template-owned: upgrades leave them alone
#   and uninstall keeps the backups on purpose.
# user-owned, NEVER touched by upgrades: everything else, notably
#   ~/.config/neva/identity.env, workspace *.local.md and USER.md,
#   all vault content folders (00-10, Food, Journal, Reviews), including the seeded
#   00 Inbox/inbox.md and 06 Memory/instincts/README.md (shipped once, then yours),
#   and vault/.obsidian: shipped once at install, then yours. This is deliberate, not an
#   omission. Buyers retheme and add plugins, and silently overwriting that on upgrade would
#   cost them more than a stale dashboard does. If a dashboard changes, docs say what to copy.
